    id: "recurring-upgrade-dedup-past-due-customer-reuse-gap",
    decision:
      "Sharing the same baseline as business-decisions-archive-20-split above (origin/main tip 8282db70, PR #927 merged behind sibling PR #928).",
    rationale:
      "Given how severe the just-shipped stripe-checkout-recurring-upgrade-dedup fix's own bug class was (silent double-billing), a research agent was dispatched to sweep the rest of the payment/checkout/subscription family for the SAME general bug shape rather than pivoting to an unrelated area. Starter-to-Growth/Complete upsell, Commerce Buy-now checkout, both Billing Portal creators, and every other Stripe-resource-creation call site (products/prices/accounts, all already idempotency-keyed from prior iterations) all checked out genuinely clean — but it found a real residual gap in the fix's own new logic, independently re-verified by reading the exact predicate definitions and call sites myself rather than trusting the report.\n\nexistingRecurringStripeCustomerId and otherLiveRecurringStripeCustomerIdsToSupersede (src/lib/orders/recurring.ts) both gated on orderHasActiveRecurringEntitlement, which requires recurringCanceledAt to be unset. But recurringCanceledAt is set by BOTH a terminal customer.subscription.deleted AND a temporary past_due/unpaid/pause_collection pause (confirmed directly: the customer.subscription.updated 'pauses' branch in the webhook route calls the exact same markOrdersRecurringCanceledByCustomer as the terminal-delete branch) — the underlying Stripe Subscription is still very much alive mid-dunning in the pause case, just not currently collecting. Consequence: if a lead's old Growth subscription happened to be past_due at the moment they completed a Complete-tier upgrade checkout, existingRecurringStripeCustomerId would not find that old Customer (the order looks 'canceled' to the CRM), so the checkout route would mint a brand-new Customer via customer_email exactly as the original bug did. Even if the webhook-side detection HAD found it, the second independent failure point was that stripe.subscriptions.list({customer, status: 'active'}) explicitly filters to status=active, which excludes a past_due Subscription by definition — so it would never have been listed to cancel regardless. If the customer later paid off the past_due invoice (dunning recovers), Stripe resumes the OLD subscription to active, and the existing customer.subscription.updated 'grants' handler (syncOrdersRecurringEntitlementByCustomer) would clear its recurringCanceledAt and resync its tier — reactivating billing on the OLD $99/mo Growth subscription now stacked alongside the NEW $199/mo Complete subscription. This reproduces the exact double-billing outcome the original fix eliminated, just reached via a payment-failure timing window instead of a same-day double-checkout — narrower than the original bug (requires the old subscription to be mid-dunning at upgrade time) but a legitimate, root-caused instance of the identical bug family.\n\nThe root cause is that recurringCanceledAt conflates two genuinely different facts: 'this lead is not currently entitled to sold monthly capabilities' (the field's primary purpose, correctly checked via orderHasActiveRecurringEntitlement everywhere that matters for entitlement) and 'the underlying Stripe resource no longer exists' (which recurringCanceledAt cannot answer on its own, since a pause sets it too). This exact ambiguity is already a named, established concern in this same file: orderEligibleForPauseNotice's own doc comment explains precisely why it must use orderHadRecurringEntitlement (ignoring recurringCanceledAt) instead of the narrower orderGrantsSoldCapabilityEntitlement for its own different reason (a write-before-read ordering issue) — this fix applies the identical, already-idiomatic escape hatch to a third call site facing the same underlying field ambiguity from a different angle.\n\nFixed by switching both existingRecurringStripeCustomerId and otherLiveRecurringStripeCustomerIdsToSupersede to gate on orderHadRecurringEntitlement instead of orderHasActiveRecurringEntitlement, and by dropping the status: 'active' filter on the webhook's stripe.subscriptions.list call entirely — Stripe's own default behavior (per the SDK's own doc comment: 'By default, returns a list of subscriptions that have not been canceled') already returns every non-terminally-canceled Subscription (active, past_due, unpaid, trialing, paused), which is exactly the set that still needs to be found and canceled. Confirmed this widening is safe for the genuinely-already-dead case too: reusing an old, fully-canceled Stripe Customer for a brand-new subscriber is harmless (Stripe Customers are cheap and reusable, never require deletion), and calling subscriptions.list for a Customer whose Subscription really is already canceled simply returns an empty list — no incorrect action is ever taken, just one extra harmless API call.\n\nUpdated the two existing tests that encoded the OLD (now-incorrect) behavior ('excludes an already-canceled prior order') to reflect the new intended semantics, and added new tests proving the past_due case specifically: a checkout-route test confirming the existing Customer is reused even when the prior order's recurringCanceledAt is set, and a webhook-route test confirming a past_due superseded subscription is still found (no status filter) and canceled with the same win-back-suppression cancellation_details.comment as before. Also fixed the one existing assertion that had hardcoded the old status: 'active' filter shape. Verified via the git-stash regression-catching technique: stashed only the two source files (recurring.ts, webhook/route.ts) and confirmed all 3 new/extended assertions failed with the expected mismatch (undefined Customer, filter still including status: 'active', zero calls for the past_due case) before restoring the fix.\n\nsrc/lib/orders/recurring.ts - the fix (both predicates + updated doc comments naming the ambiguity). src/app/api/stripe/webhook/route.ts - dropped the status filter. src/lib/orders/recurring.test.ts, src/app/api/stripe/checkout/route.test.ts, src/app/api/stripe/webhook/route.test.ts - updated/new tests. src/app/docs/architecture/page.tsx - one new paragraph. src/lib/process/business-decisions.ts - this entry. src/lib/process/prompt_process.tsx - the accompanying ITERATIONS entry (combined with the archive-split above, since both landed in the same iteration/PR). No NEXT_STEPS change - this is a correctness fix in an already-shipped pre-launch capability, not a NEXT_STEPS item; the five gated items remain operator/legal/infra-blocked. Diff-gate anchor moves to a5b0d605 (origin/main tip after sibling PR #928 merged mid-iteration).\n\nFile-size check per AGENT_PROTOCOL's re-archive note: prompt_process.tsx (~203KB) is comfortably under the 262,144-byte trigger; business-decisions.ts just dropped to ~75KB via the split above - both have ample headroom now.\n\nVerified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run targeted (recurring.test.ts, checkout/route.test.ts, webhook/route.test.ts - 334/334 passed) then full suite (3772 passed, 217 files, unchanged from post-merge baseline) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same route split, no new warnings). No dev-server smoke check needed - server-only Stripe orchestration fix with no rendered UI change beyond the docs paragraph, not meaningfully exercisable beyond automated test coverage without a live STRIPE_SECRET_KEY and a real past_due Subscription in this sandboxed environment.",
